{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact d24e5a468d3d5c08581dedaa0ed77408064812a8 for mint lane C, implementation request #9476. All 28 effective artifacts were inspected at this head, with plans/README.md as the primary. Two P2 findings remain.\n\n1. P2: Bound canonical job-token cleanup after the handoff, as well as before it. packages/room/src/jobs.ts@d24e5a46:159 reads every due job_tokens row and awaits settleToken sequentially. The query has neither a limit nor ordering by next_ms, and core.runAll awaits the jobs step before snapshots, abort, publication and the mint-ledger step. The per-token wait is now bounded, but the number of reads, writes and waits in an alarm still grows with the backlog. An independent real-Room control seeded 45 owed, unexpired canonical job tokens and ran runDurableObjectAlarm once: all 45 revoke calls were sent; the named assertion failed with expected 45 \u003c= 20. This contradicts the adopted R-MINT-7 bounded-work rule and the design's bounded-work table, and can delay the later alarm work by the sum of those waits. Preserve job_tokens ownership and expiry settlement, but service owed job credentials in indexed, bounded, earliest-due order, without waiting for the debt pass on the alarm. Keep durable eligibility during an outstanding bounded attempt, the overdue 1-second continuation, earlier unrelated alarms and fresh-object recovery. Include a real-DO backlog/held-answer control that proves later alarm work can run and the backlog progresses through alarms alone; break its batch, ordering, in-flight eligibility and continuation guards. Do not merely cap the returned projection or discard extra debt.\n\n2. P2: The commissioned source-scan guard misses a valid call outside the allowed files. packages/room/test/node/mint-sites-scan.test.ts@d24e5a46:39 detects .createToken( but misses .createToken?.(. In a disposable exact-head clone I added a production room/src file with a RepoHandle parameter and repo.createToken?.('read', 60); all three source-scan tests passed. This is a regression-enforcement gap, not a claim that this head already contains an extra provider call. Make the scan fail on this optional call and ordinary method extraction/call forms, retaining the fork exception and excluding declarations/type references. A small syntax-based scan or a conservative check for method references is simpler to defend than a regex whose syntax holes allow bypass. Add a control that scans an actual outside-allowlist source fixture, and independently demonstrate that it goes red when the enforcement is removed.\n\nWhat holds: all eight canonical sites use the Room's same ledger with their adopted scopes and lifetimes. Check jobs recompute the lifetime after the persisted wake, persist the actual asked lifetime before send, pass the absolute deadline as notAfter, and retain the dispatch boundary. The handoff writes the job token row and claims the mint in the same transaction; failed handoffs leave the ledger owning the token and release it by ID. The retained late-create/late-answer/lost-answer controls keep the deadline guarantee limited to accepted tokens actually issued to checkers. Unknown creates stay unknown past time and inventories; partial ID answers become owed. The one-time legacy move retains each open create as an unknown record. An additional independent SQLite-trigger control proved adoption, counts, deletion and marker roll back together; removing that transaction caused its named assertion to fail.\n\nArchitecture: Artroom's Git publisher/token services, Room storage/alarm composition, check-job dispatch and operational projections are affected. Relative to Gitseq's architecture layers, these remain application code and ordinary Git artifacts (layer 1), coordinated by the Workroom profile and its surfaces (layers 5-7); no kernel, live runtime or host-binding contract (layers 2-4) changes. The adopted Artroom credential contract remains protocol section 32; the unbounded post-handoff path needs repair to meet it, not a quieter redefinition of it. The client and logRemote interfaces are updated consistently at their callers and harnesses. The constructor's earlier ledger creation is sound: landing is read lazily by known(), after construction.\n\nSecurity: the four authority facts were freshly checked from the durable record: planner's effective operator foundation and ratifier projection; governing request 10fcfe4e explicitly commissions the decision with separate implementing requests; approval ad6cc052 is ratified and receipt 867e496c satisfies that decision request; current merged note artifact d19f5107 is effective, live and not stale in the fresh #9970 projection. Lane C's effective request and builder promise 98f13889 are linked to that chain, and every implementing commit has its full request trailer. No live provider authority follows. Provider token text is transient, ownership transfers atomically, cleanup addresses known IDs only, unmatched tokens are not swept, expiry is rechecked immediately before a revoke, and timed-out lookups/revocations do not apply late local changes. The scope/deadline controls hold. Existing broader provider-error sinks remain separately under d29c09fa; its repaired 18d69cda invitation is claimed, but that candidate is not reviewed or approved here. No deployment, real credential or live provider call was made.\n\nSimplification and choices: retain one ledger and one shared unknown observation. Settling an already expired job token without a fictitious revoke is consistent with R-MINT-4 and the owner's rule; the changed older tests still prove its access has ended and its row is gone. A notAfter-refused token staying owed until the next ledger pass is consistent with lane A. The constructor move transaction, fixed adoption note, earlier ledger wiring and per-object WeakMap wait hook are reasonable. Purposes distinguish the sites and owners. Four record statements per released mint are consistent with the approved post-wake lifetime persistence obligation. The report exposes 11 log tokens per failing publication retry, and the revised idle control bounds that cost per retry rather than hiding it. Carry that physical-row cost into the existing 8bd623cc accounting work; it is not grounds to weaken ownership or cut functional scope. Findings 1 and 2 remove duplicated unbounded cleanup and strengthen the required scan without adding a new product outcome.\n\nValidation at exact d24e5a46: npm ci and root typecheck exited 0; root npm test exited 0 with 2,280 passed and 1 skipped across all 14 framework summaries, including Git Node 285, Room Node 200 and Room workerd 493. Git test:workers passed 11; Room Wrangler deploy --dry-run bundled and exited without upload; the Git harness typecheck exited 0 after the scratch config included the generated Worker declarations (the initial missing-declaration run is not credited). All outputs were read. Independently applied and restored 32 runtime mutants covering the eight site routing, dropped debt, one-time move, notAfter, dispatch, post-wake lifetime, future-time preservation, claim/release failure, handoff due time, expiry recheck, bounded waits and late lookup send, safe metadata, adoption count/deadline/deletion, token lifetimes and log push scope. Each produced a named AssertionError, with no syntax/module-load failure. The added migration-transaction mutant makes 33. The restored lane C/transfer/independent migration suites passed 33 tests. The separate backlog control fails and the separate optional-call scan probe stays green, as described above. Pristine, guard and canonical tracked checkouts are clean; diff --check passes. Evidence is in /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-c-v1*.log and /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-c-v1-mutants/, with the independent controls saved outside the clones. Repairs stay on the same implementation request and promise, then republish every path at one new exact head for review. Full functional scope remains open.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b28dc990b270b4c91423e38a2bbdf2d76a7c7020","binding":"assigned","head":"d24e5a468d3d5c08581dedaa0ed77408064812a8","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2d8015c7717c78f9fc9376d2a9651e5292a76c64\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:98f13889578e939bbc365086a9a1794742e80397\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0838c28719c6b279877d751ca566a5f58a92dcce","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}